  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div",
      "value": "A budget is a great way to get control over your money and achieve your financial goals. The following 5 steps can help you create your personal budget."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/dl/div/dt",
      "value": "1. Set your financial goals"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/dl/div/dd",
      "value": "What do you want to accomplish in both the short term (one year or less) and the long term? Examples include paying down debt, establishing an emergency fund, and saving for retirement. Keep in mind that your goals will likely change over time and make updates accordingly."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/dl/div[2]/dt",
      "value": "2. Determine your net income"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/dl/div[2]/dd",
      "value": "How much do you take-home in income every month, after employer deductions for Social Security, taxes, 401(k), etc.?"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/dl/div[3]/dt",
      "value": "3. Categorize and track your spending for at least a month"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/dl/div[3]/dd",
      "value": "Classify your monthly expenses into one of two categories, those that stay the same each month (fixed) and those that change from month to month (variable). Your rent or mortgage payment is an example of a fixed expense and gas for your car is an example of a variable expense. A spreadsheet, smartphone app, or old-fashioned pen and paper will come in handy for this step. Next, record how much you spend on each and every expense using a spending journal, saving receipts, checking your account transaction history in Online Banking, or all of the above."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/dl/div[4]/dt",
      "value": "4. Tailor your budget to your needs"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/dl/div[4]/dd",
      "value": "Decide how much you want to set aside each month for savings goals and variable, considering the financial goals you set in step 1. Make adjustments as needed, looking for ways to spend less and earn more. For example, you may way to cut down on entertainment spending each month in order to free up more funds for your child's college savings fund. Moderation is key but don't be too financially conservative. Setting a budget that cuts all the fun out of your life is doomed for failure."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/dl/div[5]/dt",
      "value": "5. Review your budget monthly"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/dl/div[5]/dd",
      "value": "At the end of each month, compare your actual spending to your budget to see how things are going. Forecast you income and expenses for next month so that you don't get taken by surprise. If you are consistently going over-budget in a particular category out of necessity, consider cutting down on spending elsewhere. If you are regularly under-budget on a certain category, reduce the amount you allocate for that category accordingly."
    }
  },
